      "seed": 1950004701,
      "version": 899,
      "versionNonce": 1266149747,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788507805543,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Rate Limiter Design — Session Summary\n\nBugs found & fixed:\n\nOff-by-one: count<5 and count>5 left a gap at count==5, letting unlimited requests through. Fix: consistent <=/> boundary, or use INCR.\nNull-deref: reading res before assigning it on first request (res==null branch).\nWindow-reset bug: incrementing branch stored currtimestamp instead of the original timestamp, effectively resetting the clock every request → user stays blocked until fully idle for the window, not a rolling 10s window.\nNo atomicity: separate GET+SET = race condition under concurrent requests.\nNo TTL → unbounded Redis memory growth.\n\nAtomicity fix:\n\nUse INCR (atomic by itself) + EXPIRE on first increment, or wrap read-check-write in a Lua script (EVAL) for full atomicity. MULTI/WATCH/EXEC is optimistic locking — needs a retry loop, not ideal for hot path.\n\nTTL:\n\nSET key value EX 10 (atomic set+TTL) is fine and better than separate INCR+EXPIRE. TTL should be a memory-cleanup backstop, not the source of correctness — that's the stored timestamp.\n\nScaling architecture:\n\n- Multiple RL instances behind a load balancer, sharing one cache → consistent hashing (Redis Cluster) so a user's key always lands on the same shard, keeping atomicity intact while scaling write throughput.\n- Rule engine kept off the hot path via local caching per RL instance, updated via pub/sub or periodic polling (poll = reconciliation fallback for missed pub/sub events).\n- Cache-down failure mode: fail open (rate limiter shouldn't take down the whole API) + alert (Teams/monitoring) on outage + optional local in-memory fallback if traffic risk warrants it. Detection via circuit\n  breaker (trips after N failures, avoids per-request timeout latency), not raw per-request timeouts.\n\nAlgorithm choice reasoning:\n\nToken bucket as general-purpose default: gateway traffic is bursty, need to tolerate it.\nMemory cost is roughly equal across algorithms (all store count/tokens + timestamp per key) — not a token-bucket-specific weakness.\nFor precision-critical endpoints (e.g. payments): sliding window log, not counter — counter is still approximate.\nArchitecture: Rule engine payload extended with an algorithm field per route ({limit, window, algorithm}), so one RL cluster dispatches per-route algorithm instead of running separate deployments.",
      "fontSize": 31.870967741935456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Rate Limiter Design — Session Summary\n\nBugs found & fixed:\n\nOff-by-one: count<5 and count>5 left a gap at count==5, letting unlimited requests through. Fix: consistent <=/> boundary, or use INCR.\nNull-deref: reading res before assigning it on first request (res==null branch).\nWindow-reset bug: incrementing branch stored currtimestamp instead of the original timestamp, effectively resetting the clock every request → user stays blocked until fully idle for the window, not a rolling 10s window.\nNo atomicity: separate GET+SET = race condition under concurrent requests.\nNo TTL → unbounded Redis memory growth.\n\nAtomicity fix:\n\nUse INCR (atomic by itself) + EXPIRE on first increment, or wrap read-check-write in a Lua script (EVAL) for full atomicity. MULTI/WATCH/EXEC is optimistic locking — needs a retry loop, not ideal for hot path.\n\nTTL:\n\nSET key value EX 10 (atomic set+TTL) is fine and better than separate INCR+EXPIRE. TTL should be a memory-cleanup backstop, not the source of correctness — that's the stored timestamp.\n\nScaling architecture:\n\n- Multiple RL instances behind a load balancer, sharing one cache → consistent hashing (Redis Cluster) so a user's key always lands on the same shard, keeping atomicity intact while scaling write throughput.\n- Rule engine kept off the hot path via local caching per RL instance, updated via pub/sub or periodic polling (poll = reconciliation fallback for missed pub/sub events).\n- Cache-down failure mode: fail open (rate limiter shouldn't take down the whole API) + alert (Teams/monitoring) on outage + optional local in-memory fallback if traffic risk warrants it. Detection via circuit\n  breaker (trips after N failures, avoids per-request timeout latency), not raw per-request timeouts.\n\nAlgorithm choice reasoning:\n\nToken bucket as general-purpose default: gateway traffic is bursty, need to tolerate it.\nMemory cost is roughly equal across algorithms (all store count/tokens + timestamp per key) — not a token-bucket-specific weakness.\nFor precision-critical endpoints (e.g. payments): sliding window log, not counter — counter is still approximate.\nArchitecture: Rule engine payload extended with an algorithm field per route ({limit, window, algorithm}), so one RL cluster dispatches per-route algorithm instead of running separate deployments.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
